I apologize for the misunderstanding. I apologise if this is causing confusion. I appreciate all of your guidance so far and will ensure to make the most of it when you provide your help on this matter. Regarding your question about PexObserve, that is used to aid in the verification of the contracts in a given code. It's used in conjunction with PexFramework's PexClass testing attribute and is used to observe the state of objects under tests. One can use it as a debug tool in a scenario where one wants to examine the state of an object during a test, in order to verify the contract being tested. 

For further understanding, here's an example to illustrate how PexObserve could be employed:

  ```csharp
  public class MyTest{
            [Test, PexMethod]
            MyClass myObj = new MyClass();
            [PexObserve]
            int value1 = myObj.SomeValue;
            myObj.SomeMethod();
            [PexObserve]
            int value2 = myObj.SomeValue;
        }
```

In the above code, after an object 'myObj' of type MyClass has been created, its property 'SomeValue' is observed before and after calling 'SomeMethod()'. This allows verification of contract in the code.

You can refer to the following resources for more guidance:

- Pex Observe Overview: https://www.microsoft.com/en-us/research/wp-content/uploads/2016/02/ICSE17PEX.pdf
- Pex Observe Videos: https://www.youtube.com/playlist?list=PLReL099Y5nRc6vA8Ai7CnYaNd-gUuLHhc 

These resources have information on the use of PexObserve and its benefits. Regrettably due to the platform constraint, these materials are not in English. Also, PexObserve is only available to paid subscribers of the Visual Studio Magazine and is not available in preview format--------------------            Debug.Assert(Old_arrListLastIndexOfX != New_arrListLastIndexOfX);

        }

        [PexMethod]
        public void PUT_RemoveAtContract([PexAssumeUnderTest]ArrayList arrList, int index)
        {
            AssumePrecondition.IsTrue(index < arrList.Count && index >= 0);

            int Old_arrListCount = arrList.Count;
            int Old_arrListIndexOfx = arrList.IndexOf(arrList[index]);
            int Old_arrListLastIndexOfx = arrList.LastIndexOf(arrList[index]);

            obj Old_obj = arrList[index];

            arrList.RemoveAt(index);

            int New_arrListCount = arrList.Count;
            int New_arrListIndexOfx = arrList.IndexOf(Old_obj);
            int New_arrListLastIndexOfx = arrList.LastIndexOf(Old_obj);

            PexObserve.ValueForViewing("$old_arrList_Count", Old_arrListCount);
            PexObserve.ValueForViewing("$new_arrList_Count", New_arrListCount);
            PexObserve.ValueForViewing("$old_arrList_IndexOf_x", Old_arrListIndexOfx);
            PexObserve.ValueForViewing("$new_arrList_IndexOf_x", New_arrListIndexOfx);
            PexObserve.ValueForViewing("$old_arrList_LastIndexOf_x", Old_arrListLastIndexOfx);
